      "kind": "lede",
      "html": "<p>An EICR code is not an opinion. C1, C2, C3 and FI have fixed meanings under BS 7671. Your code decides if an installation is safe. Get it wrong and a danger stays live, or a landlord misses the 28-day deadline.</p>"
    },
    {
      "kind": "takeaways",
      "heading": "The short version",
      "html": "<ul><li>C1 means danger now. Tell the client in writing not to use it.</li><li>C2 means potentially dangerous. The EICR is Unsatisfactory.</li><li>C3 means improvement recommended. It does not make the EICR Unsatisfactory.</li><li>FI means further investigation. The EICR is Unsatisfactory until it is resolved.</li><li>In England, a landlord must fix C1 and C2 faults within 28 days, or sooner if the report says so.</li><li>Quickler shows C1 and C2 on the dashboard as you enter them. You choose the codes.</li></ul>"
    },
    {
      "kind": "default",
      "eyebrow": "The codes",
      "heading": "Four codes, one outcome",
      "html": "<p>An EICR uses a small set of fixed codes. They come from BS 7671. They are not opinion.</p><p>You cannot make a C2 into a C3 because the client seems relaxed.</p>"
    },
    {
      "kind": "default",
      "eyebrow": "C1",
      "heading": "Danger now",
      "html": "<p>C1 means danger is present and someone could be hurt now.</p><p>Examples: bare live conductors, missing protective devices, damaged wiring with bare cores, no earthing where it is needed.</p><p>Tell the client in writing not to use the affected part until it is fixed and checked. If you can make it safe on the spot, do. If you cannot, make the danger clear before you leave.</p>"
    },
